This cache is called from many threads in the default (GIL) build of CPython:

```python
cache = {}

def get(key):
    if key not in cache:
        cache[key] = expensive(key)
    return cache[key]
```

What can go wrong?

Four threads each execute this 10,000 times on a shared field that starts at 0:

```java
volatile int count;
// in each thread:
count++;
```

What final value can the program print after joining all threads?

This runs on a single JavaScript thread (Node.js):

```js
let balance = 100;
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));

async function withdraw(amount) {
  const current = balance;
  await sleep(1);
  if (current >= amount) balance = current - amount;
}

await Promise.all([withdraw(80), withdraw(80)]);
console.log(balance);
```

What does it print?

Every access to `balance` here is synchronized:

```java
class Account {
  private int balance = 100;
  synchronized int getBalance() { return balance; }
  synchronized void withdraw(int amt) { balance -= amt; }
}

// Two threads run concurrently:
if (acct.getBalance() >= 80) acct.withdraw(80);
```

Can the balance go negative?

Consider this C++ code:

```cpp
std::atomic<int>  data{0};
std::atomic<bool> ready{false};

// Thread 1
data.store(42, std::memory_order_relaxed);
ready.store(true, std::memory_order_relaxed);

// Thread 2
while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) {}
int r = data.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
```

Can `r` be 0?

This lazy singleton is used from many threads:

```java
class Config {
  private static Config instance;
  int port;
  private Config() { port = 8080; }

  static Config get() {
    if (instance == null) {
      synchronized (Config.class) {
        if (instance == null) instance = new Config();
      }
    }
    return instance;
  }
}
```

Why can a thread calling `get()` see `port == 0`?

Which three changes make the lazy singleton below correct under the Java Memory Model? (Choose three.)

```java
private static Config instance;
static Config get() {
  if (instance == null) {
    synchronized (Config.class) {
      if (instance == null) instance = new Config();
    }
  }
  return instance;
}
```

Thread A publishes an object through a plain (non-volatile) static field:

```java
class Point {
  final int x;
  int y;
  Point() { x = 1; y = 2; }
}
static Point shared;

// Thread A
shared = new Point();

// Thread B
Point p = shared;
if (p != null) System.out.println(p.x + " " + p.y);
```

Thread B sees a non-null `p`. What can it print under the Java Memory Model?

