`data` and `flag` are `Int32Array`s over SharedArrayBuffers shared between a Node.js worker and the main thread:

```js
// worker
data[0] = 42;              // plain write
Atomics.store(flag, 0, 1);

// main thread
while (Atomics.load(flag, 0) === 0) {}
console.log(data[0]);      // plain read
```

What does the main thread print?

Two Node.js workers share an `Int32Array` `view` over a SharedArrayBuffer. Each runs this loop 1,000,000 times:

```js
view[0]++;
Atomics.add(view, 1, 1);
```

After both finish, what are `view[0]` and `view[1]`?

Four threads each run this loop, and the main thread joins them all before printing:

```cpp
std::atomic<int> hits{0};

// in each thread:
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i)
    hits.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);

// after joining every thread:
std::cout << hits.load();
```

What is printed?

Store-buffering litmus test, with `x` and `y` starting at 0:

```cpp
// Thread 1
x.store(1, std::memory_order_release);
int r1 = y.load(std::memory_order_acquire);

// Thread 2
y.store(1, std::memory_order_release);
int r2 = x.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
```

Can both `r1` and `r2` be 0?

Independent reads of independent writes, with `x` and `y` starting at 0, stores using release and loads using acquire:

```cpp
// T1: x.store(1);            T2: y.store(1);
// T3: r1 = x.load(); r2 = y.load();
// T4: r3 = y.load(); r4 = x.load();
```

Can T3 see `r1 = 1, r2 = 0` while T4 sees `r3 = 1, r4 = 0`, so the two readers disagree on which write happened first?

Load-buffering litmus test, with `x` and `y` starting at 0:

```cpp
// Thread 1
int r1 = x.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
y.store(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);

// Thread 2
int r2 = y.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
x.store(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
```

Is `r1 == 1 && r2 == 1` allowed? And if the loads use acquire and the stores use release?

Consider this listener:

```java
class Listener {
  final int threshold;
  Listener(EventBus bus) {
    bus.register(this);   // another thread may call onEvent() from now on
    threshold = 10;
  }
  void onEvent() { System.out.println(threshold); }
}
```

The event-bus thread calls `onEvent()` through the registered reference. What can it print?

Built with optimizations, this C function sometimes never returns even after another thread sets `ready = 1`:

```c
int ready = 0;          /* written by another thread */

void wait_for_ready(void) {
    while (!ready) { }
}
```

What is the correct fix in C11 or C++11?

Peterson's mutual-exclusion algorithm is written with plain loads and stores, and the compiler keeps them in program order:

```c
// thread i, where j = 1 - i
flag[i] = 1;
turn = j;
while (flag[j] && turn == j) { }
/* critical section */
flag[i] = 0;
```

On x86, both threads can still enter the critical section at once. Why?

A team calls this pattern a "benign" race, because `init_once_idempotent()` is safe to run more than once:

```cpp
bool initialized = false;   // plain bool, shared

// Several threads may run this concurrently:
if (!initialized) {
    init_once_idempotent();
    initialized = true;
}
```

Under the C++ memory model, what is wrong?
